Eight findings below, most severe first. I read the diff and probed the PR branch with real ESBMC and a fake validator. I did not run the full test suite. CI is green except `codecov/patch`, which fails. I did not look into why.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 187,
    "summary": "`_int_value` calls `int(digits, 8)` on a leading-zero literal with no guard, so `08` or `09` raises an uncaught ValueError.",
    "failure_scenario": "Body tokens come from the masked text, so literals in inactive `#if 0` arms are scanned. `int f(int a) {\\n#if 0\\n return a + 09;\\n#endif\\n return a; }` makes `generate_mutants` raise `ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 8: '09'`. I reproduced this. It escapes as ValueError rather than MutationError, and the whole mutation run for that function is lost over a literal that is never compiled."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/body.py",
    "line": 190,
    "summary": "Mutation sites are taken from every preprocessor arm, so mutants of inactive `#if 0` / `#else` code are emitted and pass validation.",
    "failure_scenario": "`int f(int a){\\n#if 0\\n return a<1;\\n#else\\n return a>1;\\n#endif\\n}` yields relational-flip, const and havoc mutants for line 3, which is dead code. I reproduced this. Each dead-arm mutant parses, so the validator keeps it, but it is behaviourally identical to the original, so it can never be killed. This deflates kill rates systematically, which is not the 'occasional equivalent mutant' the module docstring disclaims. `find_definition_brace` also supports `def_line` and `predefined` anchoring, and `locate_body` uses neither."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 443,
    "summary": "The validator maps every `ListUnitsError` to 'invalid mutant', including esbmc timeouts and failures to launch it.",
    "failure_scenario": "`list_units` raises `ListUnitsError` for OSError and TimeoutExpired as well as for parse errors. A slow or overloaded esbmc (30s timeout), or a binary that vanishes mid-run, makes valid mutants return False. They are then silently counted in `dropped`, so the mutant set is biased or truncated. Only `shutil.which` at construction is treated as fatal. When a `generate_mutants` caller does not first validate the original, as `mutate_function` does, it gets an empty or short result with a high `dropped` and no error."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 287,
    "summary": "output-havoc only recognises assignments whose previous token is one of `{ ; } ) else`, so writes after a `case X:` / `default:` / label colon are never mutated.",
    "failure_scenario": "`switch(a){ case 1: *o = 2; break; default: *o = 3; }` produces no output-havoc mutants (I reproduced this), because the previous token is `:`. State-machine and decoder functions that write outputs inside switch arms silently lose the operator. Nothing reports it, and a test with `[...] >= set(applicable)` can still pass on other statements."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 153,
    "summary": "`_is_binary` treats `+`/`-` after a postfix `++`/`--` as unary, so those arithmetic sites are skipped.",
    "failure_scenario": "`return a++ + b-- - 1;` yields zero arith-swap mutants (I reproduced this). The previous token is `++` or `--`, which is not in `(')', ']')` and is not an id or num. `i++ + x` and `p-- - 1` patterns lose their `+`/`-` mutants while the docs say arith-swap covers binary `+`/`-`."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 306,
    "summary": "The output-havoc candidate's offset is the right-hand-side start, but its description line is the assignment target's line, so the two disagree for multi-line assignments.",
    "failure_scenario": "`*out =\\n    3;` gives id `output-havoc@3:5#0` but description 'havoc write through `out` ... at line 2'. The `_havoc_returns` candidate uses the expression start for both. Anything that correlates ids and descriptions, such as reports or the id-to-line mapping the docstring promises, sees inconsistent lines. The ordinal and (line, column) grouping also differs from the other operators."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 408,
    "summary": "Validation is a serial subprocess per candidate and every mutant stores a full copy of the source, so cost and memory grow with candidates x file size.",
    "failure_scenario": "With `cap=None`, a function with hundreds of candidates, such as the sha1/murmur kernels or kernel-scale files the roadmap targets, spawns one `esbmc --parse-tree-only` per candidate, one after another, each in its own tempdir. The independent checks could go through a thread pool. `kept` and `Mutant.source` hold a whole translation unit per mutant even though `edits` already encodes the diff, so thousands of mutants of a large file hold GBs. `_line_col` also rescans the source from 0 for every candidate, twice."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/mutate.py",
    "line": 535,
    "summary": "Guard-drop deletes the source span verbatim, including any preprocessor directive lines inside it, and the description echoes them.",
    "failure_scenario": "`if (a)\\n#ifdef X\\n return 1;\\n#endif\\n return 2;` gives the guard-drop description 'drop guard `if (a) #ifdef X return 1;`' (I reproduced this). The edit removes the `#ifdef` but leaves its `#endif`. The mutant is invalid and only the ESBMC oracle rejects it, so it inflates `dropped`. The raw span can also swallow a balanced directive pair and change which arm compiles. Guards whose raw span contains a directive should be skipped when the candidate is generated."
  }
]
```
